<section>
  <h2>Producer prices (PPI) — a leading indicator</h2>
  <p class="subnote">Measures prices businesses receive for goods/services before they reach consumers — often a preview of future CPI.</p>
  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">PPI, final demand, 12-mo</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--down);">4.7%</div>
      <div class="period">July 2026, down from 5.5% in June</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">PPI, monthly change</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--flat);">0.0%</div>
      <div class="period">unchanged, 2nd flat/down month running</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">PPI ex. food, energy &amp; trade, 12-mo</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--up);">4.7%</div>
      <div class="period">"core" PPI, July 2026</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:12px;">
    PPI tends to lead CPI by a month or two, since it captures price changes before they're passed on to consumers. July's flat reading — the second calm month in a row after May's sharp acceleration — came from a 0.7% drop in goods prices (crude petroleum fell nearly 12%) offsetting a 0.2% rise in services. One economist called it "good news that for a second consecutive month, PPI final demand prices have not gone up." But core PPI (excluding food, energy, and trade margins) is still running at 4.7% year-over-year, a reminder that underlying pipeline pressure hasn't fully faded even as headline PPI cools.
  </div>
  <div class="source-card" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ppi/" target="_blank">BLS — Producer Price Index (PPI)</a>
    <div class="desc">Monthly wholesale/producer-level price data, often a leading indicator for future consumer inflation.</div>
    <span class="tag">bls.gov</span>
  </div>
</section>

