      <div class="label">Airline fares, 12-mo</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--up);">+25.5%</div>
      <div class="period">July 2026, one of the largest movers in the whole basket</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="label">Airline fares, monthly</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--up);">+2.2%</div>
      <div class="period">June → July 2026</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:12px;">
    Airline fares are quietly one of the biggest year-over-year movers in the entire CPI basket, up roughly a quarter from a year ago — likely tied to jet fuel costs from the same oil disruption driving gasoline prices. It doesn't get the attention gasoline does because far fewer people buy plane tickets in a given month, but it's a real cost for anyone traveling.
  </div>
  <div class="note warn" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Tariffs:</strong> multiple sources this year — including Fed commentary and economic reporting cited elsewhere on this dashboard — point to tariffs as a contributing cost pressure, particularly on construction materials (steel, aluminum, lumber) and imported goods. BLS's CPI release doesn't break out a "tariff impact" figure directly, so this dashboard can't give you a precise number the way it can for energy or shelter. Economists and political figures disagree sharply on how large the tariff effect is versus other factors (energy, labor markets, the Iran war) — treat any specific percentage-point estimate you see elsewhere with some skepticism unless it's traceable to a named economic model, the way the war impact estimate above is.
  </div>
</section>

